# Native acceptance procedure (T09 and chunk native assertions)

`xtask native --chunk Cxx` builds the fixture-identity app with
`native-prepare` and records its executable SHA-256. Acceptance is a separate
record, `docs/planning/evidence/Cxx/native-acceptance.json`
(schema `airs-v2/native-acceptance/1`, parsed strictly by xtask), produced by
actually driving that exact executable:

- `method: "cua"` — automation through macOS accessibility/automation APIs
  against the real native window (for example REA native UI capture or System
  Events), with the captured accessibility trees/screenshots stored under
  `evidence/Cxx/native/`; or
- `method: "operator"` — a person performs the checklist on the real app and
  records observations per check.

Every check must report `pass` with an observation and evidence paths. A
`fail` or `blocked` check, a record naming different executable bytes, or a
record for the production identity fails the gate. Build success, mocked IPC
tests or reviewer scores never substitute for this record.

Prerequisites on the acceptance host: macOS grants the automation client
**Accessibility** (to read the AX tree and send key events) and, for
screenshots, **Screen Recording**. Without these grants automated acceptance is
blocked; the operator method remains available.

## Required checks

The authoritative per-chunk check list is
[native-checks.json](native-checks.json) (`airs-v2/native-checks/1`). xtask
requires the record to contain exactly those IDs, once each, with result
`pass`, a nonempty observation and at least one evidence file that the review
packet hash-binds; the record must name the fixture identity and the prepared
executable, whose current bytes are re-hashed at completion. Changing the list
is a reviewed source change (it is part of the build-flags fingerprint).

C01 covers launch/identity, native menus, keyboard-only navigation and focus,
the palette (including native navigation while it is open), unavailable
destinations, Rust-owned appearance, the accessibility tree, minimum window
size, zoom to 150%/200%, Reduce Motion, absence of Academy gamification, and
startup network behavior.

VoiceOver: C01-N08 inspects the accessibility attributes VoiceOver consumes
(AXRole, AXTitle/AXDescription, AXValue/selected state and element order) and
records exactly which were read. Spoken VoiceOver output is not claimed by an
attribute inspection; it is recorded only when an operator actually runs
VoiceOver, and otherwise remains a C24 cumulative item.
## Presentation and Academy

- **T08 Shared presentation:** web and desktop consumers build from the same
  reviewed shared source; themes/fonts/routes render correctly. General desktop
  screens mount no Academy gamification/account effects or web manager routes.
- **T09 Native UI:** actual Tauri command path, menus/shortcuts, keyboard-only
  workflow, focus restoration, VoiceOver labels/order, zoom/large text, themes,
  reduced motion, long/empty/error content, and window resizing. Mock IPC tests
  alone cannot establish these. macOS WebDriver availability must be verified;
  use supported native accessibility/automation or recorded manual steps.
- **T10 Academy identity:** device grant, pending/slow_down/expiry/cancel, refresh,
  revocation, wrong issuer/client/scope, account switch/sign-out, and stale replies.
  Token management stays Rust-owned. The learner account is independent of tenant.
- **T11 Learning authority:** client bundles contain no answer keys/private lab
  evaluation, privileged routes, or assessor tokens. Forged verdict/award/local
  exam clock cannot pass; the hosted backend decides access, grading, and awards.
  Signed-in denial differs from guest sign-in; sandbox never uses tenant secrets.
- **T12 Offline work:** download lease/revision/account access, encrypted draft
  save/restart, locked vault, sign-out/account switch, withdrawn content, duplicate
  submission IDs, changed draft versions, and reconnection. No deferred action
  replay. Valid local work remains usable without claiming offline grading.

## D058 — UI v2 "Refined Native": frozen prototype, P09 extraction plan

Accepted by the owner on 2026-10-26 ("I accept it as written"), with authority to implement P09 end to end autonomously and to proceed with C28 while C26 remains in progress; every chunk is reviewed until it scores 9 in every dimension.

The owner explored fifteen interactive shell concepts on a Claude Design canvas
(https://claude.ai/artifact/2SEXT1XMHdZkxHvdw8oaYL), selected "Refined
Native", and iterated its prototype (R2App, R2Onboard, R2Harness ×3,
R2Rationale) through sixteen independent Codex usability reviews (scores 6, 7,
7, then 8 on rounds 4–16). The prototype is frozen at canvas version 134 and
copied verbatim into `docs/architecture/ui-v2/prototype/` with a SHA-256
manifest; the canvas page is not changed again. Prototype data is invented and
is never shipped.

Decisions (grilling session, 55 questions, all recommendations accepted):

1. Extraction is spec, then TypeScript presentation, then Rust contract, as
   phase P09 chunks C27–C34 (see the chunk index). The first implementation
   slice is the shell plus the first-run wizard and add-tenant sheet.
2. The prototype's state model is normative for twelve named invariants
   (`docs/architecture/ui-v2/invariants.md`) and illustrative for code shape.
   Every prototype state field is assigned Rust-owned/observed or UI-transient
   (`state-ownership.md`); nothing goes to browser storage.
3. D014 holds: secrets enter only through the host secret sheet and vault;
   an issued API key is disclosed only through the existing `RevealSecret`
   host path, held for the active context and never persisted; key rotation
   is the admitted sequence additional key (`Version`) → local deployment
   acknowledgement → old key `Delete` with readback (no invented grace
   period or revocation); typed-name confirmation is validated in the core;
   tenant switch is refused by the core while a Harness decision is pending;
   write-capable tool calls use the native confirmation sheet.
3a. Only products with an admitted service are verified, inventoried or
   changed (Runtime Security, Gateway, Red Team and Model/skill reads).
   Agentic Identity Security and Agentic Endpoint Security render as "Not
   available in this release" with a dependency gate; no inventory,
   verification result or mutation is invented for them.
4. Tokens live in a local `--v2-*` file; Academy variables stay for the
   embedded learner view. One Rust navigation inventory feeds the native menu,
   sidebar and palette; ⌘1–⌘9 are menu-defined. Feature ids gain `home` and
   `inbox`.
5. The new shell ships behind a Rust-owned `ui_variant` flag and the legacy
   shell, flag and legacy acceptance scripts are removed in C34. The Academy
   learner view is embedded unchanged.
6. The wizard realises D013 (scan key first and only required credential);
   v1 import is offered on the tenant step.
7. Verification: Vitest fixtures named after every prototype wrapper board,
   Rust tests per invariant, native acceptance extended with focus ring, live
   regions, topmost modal and Reduce Motion persistence, screenshot evidence
   at 100%/200% in both themes per chunk. Live calls only in C29 (verify,
   Vulture, reads) and C31 (review apply, Redtail, prefixed) inside the scope
   designated 2026-10-25. C27 is reviewed at 9/10 in every dimension and the
   controller enforces it: a `docs_only` chunk has no native preparation or
   acceptance and refuses owner acceptance at completion and at recognition;
   C28–C34 follow D034.
8. Minimum window 1180×720 with the rail collapsing below 1280 wide; toasts
   persist under Reduce Motion; "Show the calls" panel shows diagnostics-
   redacted wire data; English only in P09; chunks commit on main except C34.
9. C27 proceeds in parallel with C26 (docs and controller registration only);
   C28 waits for C26.


## D059 — P09 re-cut: port the prototype, one gate per screen at 8

Accepted by the owner on 2026-10-29 after a grilling session (23 questions, all
accepted; `docs/planning/p09-recut.md`). C32 had taken twenty review rounds
while the built v2 screens never matched the design: chunks had wrapped the
legacy feature components in v2 frames instead of porting the prototype.

- The frozen prototype (D058, version 134) is the specification per screen; the
  canvas is not consulted (it is unreachable from the implementing account).
- A screen is done when it matches its board side by side at 1440×900 in both
  themes with real core data, judged by the owner from composites kept under
  the chunk's evidence; parity is structural (regions, widths, spacing, type
  scale, tokens, component shapes), content differs, accessibility
  corrections may deviate and are noted. Parity precedes review.
- One heavy gate per chunk after that approval (rebuild, native acceptance,
  the full fixture suite, the Codex review), accepted at 8 under D032/D034; the
  phase review stays 9 and every chunk accepted at 8 is raised in C44.
- v2 screens import no legacy feature view; the legacy files stay for the
  legacy shell until C44 removes it. C33 was committed without a receipt (its
  core stands; its two screens are superseded).
- Chunks: C35 shell, C36 Home, C37 Inbox fix-up, C38 Harness, C39 Administration
  (a: Runtime Security, b: other products), C40 Test Cases, C41 API Client,
  C42 Academy (prototype chrome around the hosted embed), C43 Wizard re-port
  over the existing state machine, C44 legacy removal on a branch and the P09
  review. C34 is superseded. `Subject::LAST_CHUNK` is 44.

